      <p class="eyebrow">Research Notes</p>
      <h1>Blog</h1>
      <p>Short articles on interpretable deep learning, biological data integration, and scalable neuroscience workflows.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section blog-list" aria-label="Blog posts">
      <article class="blog-card">
        <p class="post-date">March 2025</p>
        <h2><a href="#interpretable-deep-learning">Building Interpretable Deep Learning Models for Neuroscience</a></h2>
        <p>Notes on translating model explanations into hypotheses neuroscientists can test experimentally.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="blog-card">
        <p class="post-date">January 2025</p>
        <h2><a href="#ml-single-cell-genomics">Bridging Machine Learning and Single-Cell Genomics</a></h2>
        <p>A practical overview of how machine learning can help integrate single-cell RNA, ATAC, and spatial data.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="blog-card">
        <p class="post-date">November 2024</p>
        <h2><a href="#gnn-connectomics">Scaling Graph Neural Networks for Connectomics</a></h2>
        <p>Lessons from modeling neuron meshes, synapses, and large-scale circuit motifs with graph methods.</p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="section highlighted" id="interpretable-deep-learning">
      <p class="section-kicker">Featured note</p>
      <h2>Building Interpretable Deep Learning Models for Neuroscience</h2>
      <p>Interpretability is most useful when it supports a concrete experimental loop: identify features, generate hypotheses, validate with perturbation or imaging, and refine the model. My group focuses on making that loop practical for calcium imaging, spike-train analysis, and multi-modal biological datasets.</p>
    </section>
  </main>

